window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"06-04","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"06-04-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-01/","title_he":"א – המצווה לשרת בצבא – הצלת ישראל","title_en":"The Mitzva to Serve in the Army: Saving Israel","body_en":"<p>It is a great mitzva from the Torah to enlist in the Israel Defence Forces, and two mitzvot stand at the basis of the obligation, each of them a general mitzva that is equal to a certain extent to all the mitzvot: <b>first,</b> saving Israel from the hand of their enemies; <b>second,</b> guarding the Land of Israel so that it will be in the hands of the people of Israel.</p>\n<p><b>Saving Israel</b>: we were commanded that if we see one of our people in danger, we must come to his aid, as it is said (Vayikra 19:16): \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\", and for this a person must be ready to enter a certain risk. All the more so when the whole of Israel is in danger, there is an obligation to come out to save Israel. And the Sages already said in the Mishna (Sanhedrin 4:5): \"Whoever sustains one soul of Israel, Scripture accounts it to him as though he had sustained a whole world\"; if so, one who is a partner in sustaining the whole people, all the more so does he truly sustain a whole world.</p>\n<p>And this is a clear war of mitzva, as the Rambam wrote (Laws of Kings 5:1): \"And which is a war of mitzva? This is the war against the seven nations (in the conquest of the Land), the war against Amalek, <b>and helping Israel against an enemy that has come upon them</b>.\" The mitzva of war obliges each individual to take risks far beyond what we were commanded to risk for the sake of saving individuals of Israel. For the sake of saving an individual or individuals, according to all opinions there is no mitzva to enter a danger in which the rescuer will most likely be killed. But in time of war, when it is necessary to endanger individuals in order to save the campaign, the individual must be ready to enter even a situation in which his danger is greater than his chance of being saved. And as our master Rav Kook wrote (Mishpat Kohen 143), the principle \"and he shall live by them\", from which we learned that danger to life overrides all the mitzvot of the Torah, does not apply in time of war, because the laws of the public differ from the laws of the individual, and for the sake of the public's existence individuals must be ready to enter danger. On this basis the responsa 'Tzitz Eliezer' (13:100) wrote that the principle \"your life comes before the life of your fellow\" too does not apply in war, \"rather all the men of war, as one man, are obligated each and every one to give his life for the saving of the life of his comrade. And this too is included in the rules of the laws of the public, and in the category of the conduct of the state and its ordering\" (Ha-tzava Ke-halakha chapter 15).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Regarding the obligation of an individual to save his fellow there are two opinions. According to most poskim, a person need not enter mortal danger in order to save his fellow, because the principle \"and he shall live by them, and not die by them\" (Yoma 85b) applies also to the mitzva \"you shall not stand by the blood of your fellow\". That is, any action involving a real risk to life, which an ordinary person would not be ready to enter even to save his property, there is no mitzva to do in order to save one's fellow. This is the view of Rabbeinu Yona of Gerona, Sma ḤM 426:2, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav OḤ 329:8 and Laws of Bodily and Mortal Damages 7, MB 329:19, Igrot Moshe YD vol. 2, 174:2, Tzitz Eliezer vol. 9, 45, and others. And some say that in order to save a soul of Israel one must be ready to risk one's life; and so wrote the Beit Yosef ḤM 426, following the Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of the Yerushalmi, and the Ratziya inclined this way in 'Le-netivot Yisrael' vol. 1, p. 158 (Beit El edition). (And see Peninei Halakha Likkutim II 11:2-3.) But regarding war all agree that one must risk one's life in order to win."}]},{"slug":"06-04-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-02/","title_he":"ב – ישוב הארץ","title_en":"Settling the Land","body_en":"<p>We were commanded to inherit the Land and settle it, that is, that the Land be under the sovereignty of Israel and be settled by Jews throughout its length and breadth. And this mitzva is equal to all the mitzvot, as explained in the Sifrei (Re'eh, parasha 53). This mitzva overrides danger to the life of individuals, for we were commanded to conquer the Land, and the Torah did not intend that we rely on a miracle; and since in every war there are fatalities, the mitzva of conquering the Land obliges us to risk lives for it (Minḥat Ḥinukh 425, 604; Mishpat Kohen p. 327; and as explained above 3:4). All the more so is it a mitzva to fight in order to defend the regions of the Land already in our possession. For generations we had no ability to organise and build a military force, and therefore we were under duress and could not fulfil the mitzva. By the kindness of God, who remembered His people, in recent generations God has given us the strength to fulfil the mitzva.<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>I placed the foundation of saving the people of Israel before the mitzva of settling the Land, as did Yoav ben Tzeruya, commander of the army of Israel in the days of King David: when the battle grew fierce and there was a need to strengthen greatly against the enemy, he placed the saving of the people before settling the Land and said: \"Be strong, and let us strengthen ourselves for our people and for the cities of our God, and the Lord will do what is good in His eyes\" (II Shmuel 10:12). And so in halakha the existence of the people comes before the mitzva of settling the Land (above 3:4).</p>\n<p>The need to rely on an army in order to fulfil the mitzva of settling the Land is not an expression of weakness; on the contrary, it expresses that this people Israel merits to reveal the word of God with all its powers through natural reality. And so we find in the Midrash: \"There were four kings… David said (Tehillim 18:38): I pursue my enemies and overtake them, and I do not turn back until they are consumed; and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him and helped him. Asa arose and said: I have no strength to kill them, but I pursue them and You kill them; and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him and killed his enemies. Yehoshafat arose and said: I have no strength either to kill or to pursue, but I sing song and You kill my enemies; and the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him and killed his enemies. Ḥizkiyahu arose and said: I have no strength either to kill or to pursue or to sing song, but I sleep on my bed and You act. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: I act\" (Eikha Rabba, Petiḥta 30). Those of little faith may think that Ḥizkiyahu was the greatest of them, but the truth is the reverse. The Midrash expresses the terrible decline of Israel, from the great days of King David until close to the destruction. In the days of Ḥizkiyahu reality no longer matched the divine values, and he needed a miracle in order to survive; but a miracle can sustain only for a time, and if they do not repair nature and sanctify it, destruction will come and they will be exiled from the Land.</p>\n<p>That is, the repaired state is that the people of Israel fulfils the mitzvot of God, settles the Land and defeats the wicked through the natural human powers God has given it, without need of a miracle. Thus it will be revealed that God is One in the heavens above and on the earth below and the whole earth is full of His glory, and the earthly and human powers too reveal the word of God and do not oppose it. Out of this the world will go on rising, and all the nations will recognise the right of Israel to its Land, and we will settle the Land without need of wars.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"According to the Ramban (Additions to the positive mitzvot 4) this is a war of mitzva, to conquer the Land, and this is the view of most of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim. And see the book 'Ha-tzava Ke-halakha' by Rav Kaufman pp. 7-10. And the Rambam did not count the mitzva of settling the Land among his mitzvot, and also in the mitzvot of war he did not mention that there is a mitzva to conquer the Land, but mentioned as a mitzva only the war against the seven nations and Amalek. But the responsa Devar Yehoshua (vol. 2, OḤ 48) wrote that all this applies specifically to taking the Land out of the hands of the gentiles; but after it is in our hands, the Rambam too agrees that one must fight against them and not let them conquer even a small part of the Land. For according to the Rambam (Laws of Idolatry 10:6) the prohibition \"they shall not dwell in your land\" (Shemot 23:33) is said of all the nations, and even according to the Ra'avad the prohibition \"lo teḥonem\" applies to all the nations that have not accepted the seven mitzvot before three ḥaverim out of faith in the Lord, the God of Israel, as the Rambam wrote (Kings 8:10-11). And Rav Rabinovitch in his book 'Melumdei Milḥama' siman 1 explained that according to the Rambam, war to save part of the Land of Israel is included in the war of mitzva of \"saving Israel from an enemy\", for he ruled in Laws of Shabbat (2:23) that one must fight even on Shabbat to save even straw and stubble in a town near the border. And according to him, the only way according to the Rambam to restore Israeli sovereignty over the Land is by way of gradual settlement with the permission of the gentiles, until the establishment of a state is achieved, as indeed happened in practice."}]},{"slug":"06-04-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-03/","title_he":"ג – מצווה כללית ואיסור הפחד","title_en":"A General Mitzva, and the Prohibition of Fear","body_en":"<p>In the mitzva of military service the general side in every individual of Israel stands out. We fulfil all the mitzvot as individuals. True, all Israel are responsible for one another, and the supreme intention in all the mitzvot is to fulfil them \"in the name of all Israel\"; but the mitzva is the obligation of the individual and he fulfils it as an individual. In the mitzva of military service, however, the individual to a large extent sets aside his individuality and becomes part of the whole, and so all fight as one man for the sake of the whole of Israel.</p>\n<p>Through this the individual can avoid being afraid of the terror of war. If he weighs the mitzva as an individual, he will be afraid. And indeed in ordinary life a person must be careful and wary of dangers, for through this he can fulfil the mitzva of guarding his soul greatly. And so is the halakha: danger to life overrides all the mitzvot of the Torah (apart from the three grave ones). But in war every Jew must rise above perceiving himself as an individual, and see himself as part of the whole of Israel, as a representative of the people of Israel, which is destined to reveal the word of God to the world; and so he can overcome natural fear and fight bravely.</p>\n<p>And so the Rambam wrote (Laws of Kings 7:15): \"And once a person enters the thick of battle, let him rely on the Hope of Israel and its Saviour in time of trouble, and let him know that he is waging war for the unity of the Name; let him put his life in his hand, and not be afraid or fear, and not think of his wife or of his children. Rather let him wipe their memory from his heart and turn from everything to the war. And whoever begins to think and brood in the battle and to alarm himself transgresses a negative commandment, as it is said (Devarim 20:3): Let not your heart faint; do not fear, and do not tremble, and do not be terrified because of them. And not only this, but all the blood of Israel hangs on his neck. And if he did not win and did not wage war with all his heart and all his soul, he is like one who shed the blood of all, as it is said (ibid. 8): lest his brothers' heart melt as his heart. And it is stated explicitly in the tradition (Yirmiyahu 48:10): Cursed be he who does the work of the Lord deceitfully, and cursed be he who keeps back his sword from blood. And whoever fights with all his heart without fear, and his intention is only to sanctify the Name, is assured that he will find no harm and no evil will reach him, and he will build himself a secure house in Israel and acquire merit for himself and his children forever, and merit the life of the world to come, as it is said (I Shmuel 25:28-29): For the Lord will certainly make my lord a sure house, because my lord fights the battles of the Lord, and evil shall not be found in you all your days… and the soul of my lord shall be bound in the bundle of life with the Lord your God.\"<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The soldiers of the house of David would write a get for their wives before going out to battle (Shabbat 56a), so that if they died and were lost, their wives would not be agunot. And our master the Re'aya added in Ein Aya there, section 61, that this expresses their passage from the state of an individual family man to the level of the whole of Israel."}]},{"slug":"06-04-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-04/","title_he":"ד – מצוות הגיוס לצה\"ל","title_en":"The Mitzva of Enlisting in the IDF","body_en":"<p>The purpose of the IDF is to save Israel from the hand of their enemies, and to fulfil the mitzva of settling the Land by guarding Israeli sovereignty in the Land of Israel. Therefore it is a mitzva for every single one of Israel who is required for it to enlist in the army. And although all who enlist in the army fulfil a mitzva, there is a difference in the value of the mitzva between combat soldiers and combat support, who need to risk their lives, and soldiers in the rear.</p>\n<p>And one should not learn from the guards of the camp in David's army, with whom David determined that the spoil be divided equally with the fighters, as it is said (I Shmuel 30:24): \"For as is the share of him who goes down to the battle, so shall be the share of him who stays by the baggage; they shall share alike.\" For there, those who stayed by the baggage, that is, who guarded the camp, were also combat soldiers. But it was necessary to allot soldiers to guard the camp and the equipment, and had they not given them half the spoil, since enlistment in the army was based to a large extent on volunteering and on the prospect of receiving spoil, they would not have agreed to fill their role. In addition, they too were in danger, because in the past the rear was closer to the front, and enemy forces would always try to attack the camp in order to destroy the infrastructure of the war and decide the battle. Therefore the role of the guards of the camp was important and dangerous.</p>\n<p>But most of the rear soldiers in our day take almost no part in war, and therefore the virtue of their mitzva is lower than the virtue of those who serve in the combat units. And the rule regarding soldiers who serve in the rear is that the more the soldier contributes to the security of Israel, the greater the virtue of his mitzva, and some of them contribute enormously.</p>\n<p>However, one can learn for our day from King David's determination that one should not divide and set up levels among the combat soldiers, their corps and units, for they are all equal in importance, and all of them together are needed for war, and together they share in the honour of the fighters of Israel. Indeed, one who is in practice called upon more to risk his life for the whole of Israel merits a greater level of self-sacrifice, and according to the investment and the risk is the reward (Avot 5:23).<sup>4</sup></p>\n<p>Already in enlistment and training the soldiers fulfil the mitzva. For because our enemies know that the people of Israel has a strong army, they are sometimes deterred from fighting us. It follows that the very enlistment and training benefit the security of Israel and the fulfilment of the mitzva of settling the Land. And therefore a soldier who is killed in training too is considered as one killed for the sanctification of the Name, the people and the Land.</p>\n<p>Although many have justified complaints about various commanders and about security policy, this does not change the mitzva to enlist in the army. For after all the criticisms, without the IDF our enemies would rise up against us to destroy us. Moreover, one can say that the people of Israel never had a perfect army, without any operational failures and moral flaws. Sometimes the army had more failures and sometimes fewer. And nevertheless the mitzva to fight for the people and the Land stood firm. And when we did not succeed in fulfilling this great mitzva, terrible troubles came upon us. Therefore, when there is criticism of the IDF, one should voice it in order to improve and repair the situation, but all the criticisms do not cancel the fundamental mitzva to enlist in the army.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"This custom of dividing the spoil between those who go out to battle and those who guard the baggage began with Avraham our forefather, who after his war against the four kings gave up his share of the spoil, but insisted on the share of those who stayed by the baggage, as it is said (Bereshit 14:24): \"Save only what the young men have eaten, and the share of the men who went with me, Aner, Eshkol and Mamre; let them take their share.\" And the Sages explained that Aner, Eshkol and Mamre stayed by the baggage (brought in Rashi there). And so God commanded Moshe after the victory over Midyan that the spoil be divided in two, half to be received by the fighters and half by the rest of the people, as it is said (Bamidbar 31:27): \"And divide the spoil between those skilled in war who went out to battle and all the congregation.\" And there twelve thousand received half, and close to six hundred thousand the other half. So there is a great difference between the fighters and the rest of the people. And the Sages said (Pesikta Zutrata, Lekaḥ Tov on Bereshit there) that the things established in the Torah were forgotten, and David established them again."}]},{"slug":"06-04-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-05/","title_he":"ה – האם לומדי תורה מצווים להתגייס","title_en":"Are Those Who Learn Torah Commanded to Enlist?","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of Torah study does not override the mitzva of enlisting in the army in order to save Israel from their enemies. The Sages said (Sota 44b) that all those mentioned in the Torah as returning from the war, such as one who built a house and did not dedicate it, or planted a vineyard and did not redeem it, or betrothed a wife and did not take her, refers only to an optional war. But in a war of mitzva, such as a war to save Israel from an enemy, \"all go out, even a groom from his chamber and a bride from her canopy.\" And so ruled the Rambam (Laws of Kings 7:4).</p>\n<p>And so we find that the disciples of Yehoshua bin Nun and of King David would go out to war and were not concerned about neglect of Torah. And the angel's rebuke of Yehoshua for neglect of Torah (Megilla 3a) was about the fact that at night, when they were not occupied with the needs of the war, they neglected Torah. But for the time of training and war they were not rebuked. And it is indeed told that when Amasa needed to mobilise soldiers, he came to the Sages, and since he found them occupied with Torah, according to the halakha he could not mobilise them (Sanhedrin 49a). But there it was an optional war, which does not override Torah study. But when there is a need to mobilise those who learn Torah for a war of mitzva, such as saving Israel from an enemy, there is an obligation to set aside Torah study. And what they said (Bava Batra 8a), that the Sages do not need guarding and therefore need not bear the burden of guard duty, refers to guarding to prevent theft only. But when Israel must be defended, there is a mitzva to save, as it is said (Vayikra 19:16): \"You shall not stand by the blood of your fellow.\" And although they said (Megilla 16b), \"Torah study is greater than saving lives\", the intention is that its value is greater; but in terms of obligation, when there is a mitzva that others cannot fulfil, every mitzva overrides the mitzva of Torah study, and all the more so the mitzva of saving Israel from an enemy.</p>\n<p>However, regarding individuals capable of rising in Torah for the sake of the whole of Israel, our teacher and master Rav Tzvi Yehuda Ha-kohen Kook zt\"l taught that as long as the army is not compelled to mobilise them, it is better that they defer enlistment and rise in Torah study, and learn in order to teach Torah to the public; and through this they will make a decisive contribution to strengthening Israeli consciousness, to the security of Israel and to settling the Land. And on condition that they do this with great honour for the soldiers who fulfil with their bodies the mitzvot of saving Israel and settling the Land, because only in this way can they contribute to the elevation of the spirit and the valour of the whole of Israel.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Our teacher and master the Ratziya clarified this topic, as brought in 'Le-netivot Yisrael' vol. 1, essays 20-23, and in detail in a note on pp. 166-167 (Beit El edition). According to all opinions a war to save Israel is a war of mitzva, as the Rambam said. And according to the Ramban and those who hold like him, there is a mitzva to fight also in order to conquer the Land of Israel. And see there at the end of vol. 2, p. 607, the story of Rav She'ar Yashuv Ha-kohen, who enlisted in the army in the War of Independence with the encouragement of his father, Rav Ha-nazir. And he told the Ratziya that it was being publicised in the name of our master the Re'aya, on the basis of what he wrote in Igrot Ha-Re'aya vol. 3, 810, that it is forbidden to mobilise yeshiva students into the army. And the Ratziya was shocked and said that there reasons were brought for exempting yeshiva students from enlistment in the British army in the First World War, but they have no connection at all to a war of mitzva for the Land of Israel and the saving of Israel. Following this the Ratziya printed his clarifications on this topic. Similarly wrote Rav Rabinovitch in 'Melumdei Milḥama' pp. 7-8, and Rav Gershuni in Teḥumin 4 p. 67. However, the Ratziya was careful to teach that when there is no necessity to mobilise all the young men, then it is right that those who learn in order to teach defer enlistment. And so did most of his students in the Merkaz Ha-rav yeshiva, who deferred their enlistment for many years and served a short service; and in the end it became clear that the contribution of their learning to the security of Israel was enormous, and their students in the pre-army academies and the hesder yeshivot are among the best soldiers in the IDF.<br>On the importance of the value of the mitzva of military service I will bring here a story I heard from Dr. Feingold about the great Gaon Rav Yitzḥak Ze'ev Gustman zt\"l, the last of the Vilna geonim, who was on the beit din of the Gaon Rabbi Ḥayim Ozer Grodzinski, went through the horrors of the Holocaust and lost there his only son. Years later he founded a yeshiva in Reḥavia in Jerusalem. Among those close to Rav Gustman were Prof. Aumann (Nobel prize laureate) and his son Shlomo Aumann, may God avenge his blood, who was a student of the hesder yeshiva in Sha'alvim.<br>Shlomo was killed in the Peace for Galilee war. After the news of Shlomo's fall became known, Dr. Feingold came to take Rav Gustman to the funeral. After the funeral ended, Rav Gustman walked around among the fresh graves of the soldiers who had fallen, sighed and grieved over them, and found it hard to leave the cemetery. On their way back from the funeral he said, \"They are all holy.\" One of the passengers sitting in the back seat asked: \"All of them? Even the non-religious?\" Rav Gustman turned to the back seat and said emphatically: \"All of them! All of them!\" As they approached Reḥavia, Rav Gustman turned and said (in Yiddish): \"Dr. Feingold, perhaps we will go up to Prof. Aumann to say a word.\" As they went up he was wholly sunk in thought. When they entered the house, Rav Gustman turned quietly and with enormous feeling and said in Yiddish: \"Dr. Feingold, you did not know that I had a son, Meirke; he was a very beautiful child; I do not say this because he was my son, truly he was such a beautiful child…\" Then the Rav remembered that he was speaking in Yiddish, and repeated his words in Hebrew so that the mourning family would understand. And he turned to the widow, to the parents, to the brothers and sisters, and said: \"My son Meirke was taken from my hands and thrown onto a truck in the Kinderaktion (the children's action). And next to me stood the wife of the mashgiaḥ, and they took her child too. And then she turned to the Nazi and said: Throw me too onto the truck. The Nazi answered her: Wait, madam, your turn will come… The sounds that came from the dying and living children on the truck were nothing compared with the screams of the mothers…\"<br>And he went on to tell that after his son was murdered, in order to obtain a little food to save themselves from starvation, he took his son's small shoes in order to sell them when he went out of the ghetto for forced labour. Outside he managed to exchange them for carrots and flour. But each day he gave only one shoe, for he could not smuggle in at once all the food he would receive for both shoes. He described how he put the flour into his tied trousers, and put the carrots in slices into his shoes. When he returned to the ghetto, he went to bring part of the food to the widow of the Gaon of the generation, Rabbi Ḥayim Ozer Grodzinski, who was swollen from cold and hunger. The Rebbetzin at first refused to accept the food, for she said: \"You have a wife and a daughter; why should you give me the food?\" When he answered her, \"I have enough\", she agreed to accept from him a little carrot and flour. Rav Gustman added: \"I did not taste from this food, because I could not fulfil in myself the verse: And you shall eat the flesh of your sons (Vayikra 26:29).\"<br>And then the Rav straightened up, spread his arms out to the sides and said: \"Now I will tell you what is happening in the World of Truth. My Meirke says to Shlomo: Happy are you, Shloimele, that you merited. I did not merit! I did not merit to cast myself away in order to save the whole of Israel. You merited!\" Prof. Aumann rose from the floor, embraced Rav Gustman and said: \"You have comforted me, you have comforted me.\"<br>When Dr. Feingold's sons approached the age of enlistment, he asked Rav Gustman, who was revered by all the great rabbis, including those of the Ḥaredi circles: \"What is written in the Torah of Moshe: does one go to the army or not?\" Rav Gustman answered: \"In the Torah of Moshe our teacher it is written: Shall your brothers go to war, and shall you sit here?!\" (So Moshe our teacher said to the children of Reuven and Gad, Bamidbar 32:6.) And then he walked back and forth in the room, with his right hand half raised like a leader, and repeated the verse again and again, each time in a louder voice."}]},{"slug":"06-04-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-06/","title_he":"ו – מצוות הצניעות בצבא","title_en":"The Mitzva of Modesty in the Army","body_en":"<p>There is a whole passage in the Torah about the character of the camp of Israel, which must be holy and pure (Devarim 23:10-15): \"When you go out as a camp against your enemies, you shall guard yourself from every evil thing. If there is among you any man who is not clean by reason of what happens to him by night, he shall go outside the camp; he shall not come within the camp. But when evening comes, he shall bathe himself in water, and when the sun is down he may come within the camp. You shall have a place outside the camp, and you shall go out there. And you shall have a spade among your gear, and when you sit down outside, you shall dig with it, and turn back and cover your excrement. For the Lord your God walks in the midst of your camp, to deliver you and to give up your enemies before you; therefore your camp shall be holy, that He see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.\"</p>\n<p>In general, the Torah's guidance to the soldiers in the camp is to guard themselves \"from every evil thing\", that is, from all the transgressions in the Torah, including the transgressions considered especially evil, such as idolatry, forbidden relations and bloodshed. And from the fact that the Torah said \"evil <b>thing</b>\" (davar, also \"word\"), we learn that one must be especially careful about transgressions of speech. And the Sages explained that the intention is to hint at the prohibition of cursing the Name and the prohibition of lashon ha-ra (Sifrei there). And the Sages already said (Arakhin 15b): \"Whoever speaks lashon ha-ra increases sins corresponding to three transgressions: idolatry, forbidden relations and bloodshed.\"</p>\n<p>But in detail the Torah emphasised guarding oneself in the camp in matters of modesty, for this is the detailed intention of \"evil thing\": that one must guard oneself from sinful thoughts, as brought in the Talmud (Avoda Zara 20b): \"Our Rabbis taught: You shall guard yourself from every evil thing: that a person should not have thoughts by day and come to impurity at night.\" And so it is implied from the end of the passage that the principal intention is to warn against thoughts of ervah, as it is said: \"that He see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.\" Included in this is being careful about obscene speech and frivolity, for these prohibitions are connected both to speech and to matters of ervah. And from the words of the Sages we learn that crude talk and frivolity may cause the fall of lives of Israel (Shabbat 33a; see Tanḥuma Ki Tetze 3).</p>\n<p>Several reasons were given for this special command, and all of them are the words of the living God. The reason mentioned in the Torah is \"for the Lord your God walks in the midst of your camp\": Israel are the children of the Holy One, blessed be He, and the soldiers going out to fight for Israel are dear to Him, and therefore He rests His Shekhina among them in order \"to deliver you and to give up your enemies before you\". So the camp is holy, in a manner like the holiness of the Temple, and the soldiers must sanctify themselves in a manner like the kohanim entering the holy service. But if He sees an unseemly thing in the camp, He will remove His Shekhina from us. And Rashi added and explained, following the Sages, that it is specifically soldiers who need more help from Heaven, for the Satan accuses at a time of danger, and therefore they must be more careful about every evil thing.</p>\n<p>And the Ramban explained that the customs of soldiers in camps are well known, unrestrained in all prohibitions. Apparently soldiers who are under the mental pressure of hard training and dangers want to release themselves from the pressure, and the easy way to do so is through levity, obscene speech and preoccupation with matters of forbidden relations. In addition, the need to fight while risking lives breaks down boundaries: suddenly something as terrible as killing people becomes permitted. Suddenly the soldier discovers within himself mental strengths and powers he did not know, and if he is not careful to fence himself in, these life forces can tend in negative directions. And therefore the Torah comes to warn the soldiers to guard themselves from every evil thing.</p>\n<p>Apart from this, when a person is with his family he fences himself in more from matters of forbidden relations and licentiousness, and when he departs to the army all the usual arrangements are broken, and the concern of the fences being breached grows. In addition, there is a concern that there will be soldiers who, through great preoccupation with army matters, in training, guard duty and operations for the people and the Land, will come to disregard personal mitzvot, such as guarding the mouth and the mind from sinful thoughts. And therefore the Torah comes to tell us that the camp of Israel must be holy, and it is precisely by the merit of its holiness that we will win in wars. And also after the war the soldiers must build their families, and if they harm their modesty and holiness they will cause themselves mental damage, and will not be able to love their spouse completely. For modesty and holiness are the foundation of building love in the family.</p>\n<p>It follows, then, that the rule is that in the army one must be especially careful about the prohibitions of forbidden relations, obscene speech and sinful thoughts.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-04-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-07/","title_he":"ז – דברים שבקדושה במחנה","title_en":"Matters of Holiness in the Camp","body_en":"<p>In addition to the value of modesty for soldiers, we have learned from the passage of the camp that one must keep the camp clean, free of excrement and filth. And therefore the Torah commanded that one who needs to relieve himself go out of the camp and relieve himself, and afterwards cover his excrement. As it is said (Devarim 23:13-15): \"You shall have a place outside the camp, and you shall go out there. And you shall have a spade among your gear, and when you sit down outside, you shall dig with it, and turn back and cover your excrement. For the Lord your God walks in the midst of your camp, to deliver you and to give up your enemies before you; therefore your camp shall be holy, that He see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.\" For as long as there is excrement in the camp, it is forbidden to say matters of holiness in the camp, and God who walks in the midst of the camp of Israel may remove His Shekhina from the camp. So in addition to care in matters of modesty, one must be careful to engage in the camp in matters of holiness: to keep the prayers, to say all the blessings and to learn as much as possible. For the aim of the Torah's command to keep the camp clean is that matters of holiness can be said in it.</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Megilla 3a) that the angel of God who revealed himself to Yehoshua rebuked him because at night, when they were not occupied with preparations for war, they neglected Torah study. And indeed afterwards, on the night before the battle of Ai, Yehoshua learned Torah, as it is said (Yehoshua 8:9; 8:13): \"And Yehoshua lodged that night among the people\", \"And Yehoshua went that night into the midst of the valley\", and the Sages said (Megilla 3b): \"This teaches that he lodged in the depth of halakha.\"</p>\n<p>Indeed, not every soldier is capable of reaching the level of Yehoshua bin Nun and engaging in the depth of halakha on the night before battle. And also during training it is not easy to learn diligently. In any case, every soldier must be careful to keep the prayers and try to learn Torah according to his strength, and at the very least a few minutes every day, thereby expressing the holiness of the camp.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-04-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-08/","title_he":"ח – האם בצבא צריכים להקל או להחמיר","title_en":"Should One Be Lenient or Stringent in the Army?","body_en":"<p>As is known, sometimes in halakha there are differences of opinion, with some lenient and some stringent. Usually the halakha is decided according to the majority opinion, and in a time of pressing need one relies on the lenient opinion. And those who beautify the mitzvot try to be stringent even according to the minority opinion, in order to fulfil the obligation according to all the poskim. The question is how it is proper to conduct oneself in the army: whether to incline to leniency or to stringency.</p>\n<p>We find, on one hand, that the Torah warned us to guard ourselves in the camp from every evil thing, as it is said (Devarim 23:10): \"When you go out as a camp against your enemies, you shall guard yourself from every evil thing.\" And as we learned (halakha 6), there are three principal reasons for this: a) the Shekhina rests in the midst of the camp of Israel; b) the Satan accuses at a time of danger, and therefore specifically in the army one must be more meticulous in the mitzvot in order to be worthy of God's help; c) army life may lower the spiritual level of the soldier, and against this he must strengthen himself more in keeping the mitzvot.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, the Sages exempted the soldiers going out to fight, from the time they go out to war until their return, from six things. These are: a) they may bring wood from any place without concern for theft; b) they exempted them from seeking water for washing hands before eating bread; c) they permitted soldiers to eat demai fruits, that is, fruits that the Sages required to be tithed out of doubt; in the camp they exempted the soldiers from this; d) they exempted them from setting aside food for two meals for the sake of eruv ḥatzerot and eruv teḥumin; e) soldiers may camp in and pass through any field without concern that they will cause damage to the owner of the field; f) soldiers who were killed are buried in the place where they fell (Eruvin 17a). And all this was permitted specifically when going out to war. But during training the Sages did not permit these six things. Also in permanent outposts, when the soldiers are engaged in guarding the border, according to Rav Herzog (Heikhal Yitzḥak OḤ 47) they did not permit these six things.</p>\n<p>These six things were permitted even in a situation where there is no concern of danger to life, for in a situation of danger to life all the mitzvot are set aside, not only these six things. But the Sages saw that if the soldiers had to be careful about the six things in the hard conditions of the field, a great burden would be placed on them, which indirectly might harm their ability in battle, and therefore they exempted them. For example, if they had to carry on their backs everywhere water for washing hands and wood for lighting a fire, they would be greatly delayed. And likewise if they had to bury every fallen man in an orderly cemetery, they would sometimes have to carry him on their backs for days, and would not be free to deal with the enemy. And likewise if they had to ask permission everywhere from the landowners to camp in their territory and take wood, they would be greatly delayed. Therefore the Sages revoked the rights of the owners of the land and the wood in favour of the soldiers. It should be noted, however, that today in most of these things one is not lenient. For example, since the army has vehicles, every fallen man can be brought to a cemetery. And likewise one can easily bring abundant water for washing hands. And since there are means of communication, one can coordinate the place of camping in advance without harming the landowners.<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>In any case, we learn a principle from these foundations. On one hand, in things that may delay and disrupt military activity, one should rely on the lenient opinions, as the Sages were lenient in the six things. And on the other hand, in things that are not liable to disrupt military activity, one should be meticulous and sanctify oneself in the camp. All the more so in a time of training, when one should be more stringent and careful than at other times.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"On all this see Rav Kaufman's book 'Ha-tzava Ke-halakha' chapter 11. It should be added that from the law of the beautiful captive woman too (Devarim 21:11) one can learn that one must be lenient in the army in what is very hard to be stringent about. And it should be noted that the exemption of the camp is specifically for ten soldiers, who are called a camp, but for fewer than ten there is no exemption. And perhaps one can explain that a group of soldiers, if it had to be careful about the six things, would devote too much time to them and this might harm their preparation for battle. And this is because when they are ten they will naturally tend to be stringent, as people always tend to be more stringent in public; therefore even when the trouble of their carefulness would harm their preparation for battle, they would not rule leniently for themselves, and so the Sages exempted them from these six things, so that they would not need to deliberate over each of these laws when it came before them. But when they are fewer than ten, they were not concerned, and left the law according to the basic consideration, that concern of danger to life overrides mitzvot.<br>Among the six things there are three special enactments exempting the soldiers from grave matters: infringing on ownership of land and wood, and burying a fallen man in the place where he fell. And today, when it is possible, one is stringent in them (and see regarding burial Meshiv Milḥama vol. 2, 143). And some of them are light matters, forbidden rabbinically, which the Sages permitted to soldiers, namely separating demai and setting aside food for eruv ḥatzerot and teḥumin, and even today they remain permitted. And they disagreed regarding teḥumin what the permission is: whether they may go four thousand amot in every direction, or only in one direction; and according to Rav Goren they permitted up to twelve mil. And regarding washing hands, the exemption remains as it was, for the washing too is a rabbinic enactment. And when they have water, according to most poskim they are already obligated by the basic law. Today, since in practice water is usually available, there is no room for the permission. But even today, when water is not available, one need not seek it up to a mil. And according to many poskim they should wrap their hands in a napkin so as not to touch the bread."},{"n":7,"en":"In military service today a situation has arisen in which the secular atmosphere may influence the religious soldier, and in order to emphasise the holiness of the camp it is right to be careful to wear one's tzitzit out during army service.<br>There are young men who in their parents' home eat meat of ordinary kashrut, and in the yeshiva eat 'ḥalak' meat, and likewise in the home they intend to establish they will eat 'ḥalak'. And it is proper that in the army too they request to eat 'ḥalak'. First, because this is the most important stringency in the laws of kashrut, for according to the stringent, meat that is not 'ḥalak' is not kosher to eat. Second, under the present conditions of the army, one who is stringent to eat 'ḥalak' meat receives all the religious services in an orderly way. By ordering 'ḥalak' the military rabbinate knows about him and gives him close accompaniment, which is not so for one who is not careful about this. And even if sometimes they do not bring him 'ḥalak', it is better that he refrain from eating meat for several days in order to receive 'ḥalak' meat afterwards. However, in time of war, when it is hard to organise orderly supply, it is preferable to incline to leniency and rely on the lenient opinions, in order to enable the soldiers to strengthen themselves towards battle.<br>Sometimes the cooks in the army are gentiles, and since the basis of the prohibition of bishul akum is rabbinic, and when the cook is a gentile it is hard to be careful about this, and insisting on it may harm the fabric of relations among the soldiers, in my humble opinion one should follow the lenient opinions. a) Some say that if the gentile cooks in a Jewish home there is no concern of intermarriage and the dish is permitted (Ra'avad, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, and see Beit Yosef YD beginning of siman 113). b) Some say that if the gentile works for hire there is no concern of intermarriage and the dish is permitted (Ramban). And moreover there are those lenient regarding gentiles who are not idolaters (R. Refael Berdugo, though almost no one agreed with his view). And see Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:54. And therefore even during training one may be lenient after the fact if the cook is a gentile, and they should try to have a Jew light the fire; but after the fact, when he did not light the fire, they may eat on the basis of the lenient opinions (unless the military rabbi forbids it)."}]},{"slug":"06-04-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-09/","title_he":"ט – פקודה כנגד מצוות התורה","title_en":"An Order Against the Mitzvot of the Torah","body_en":"<p>The IDF and its commanders, acting by the authority of the public's elected representatives, have the authority to mobilise people and organise them in a military framework in order to guard the people and the Land. Sometimes there is a need to open a defensive war, and the soldiers must obey the battle orders even when this involves risking their lives. For the sake of organising the army and preparing it for battle, the army trains the soldiers. During training too the soldiers must obey orders, in order to become accustomed to building the military units and preparing them for battle.</p>\n<p>But if a soldier receives an order that contradicts the halakha, he is forbidden to carry it out. And even at the time when Israel had a king, chosen by a prophet and the Sanhedrin, if he gave an order to violate one of the mitzvot of the Torah, it was forbidden to listen to him. And so it is ruled in the Rambam (Kings 3:9): \"One who disregards the king's decree because he was occupied with mitzvot, even a minor mitzva, is exempt; the words of the master (the Holy One, blessed be He) and the words of the servant (the king): the words of the master come first. And needless to say, if the king decreed to cancel a mitzva, he is not listened to.\"</p>\n<p>For example, on Shabbat, if the commander gave an order to drive for the sake of danger to life, such as for guarding, it is a mitzva to drive. But if he gave an order to drive for another need, it is forbidden to drive. Sometimes the soldier has a doubt: perhaps there is an aspect of danger to life in the order and perhaps not. In such a situation one should carry out the order, because even a doubt of danger to life overrides Shabbat (Yoma 83a). But after Shabbat the soldier should look into the order, and if it becomes clear to him that the order was given not for the sake of danger to life, he must complain about the commander in every possible way, in order to prevent the recurrence of cases of orders being given that involve desecration of the holy Shabbat.</p>\n<p>There was a case in which soldiers received an order to drive on Shabbat in order to evacuate a Jewish outpost that had not been approved by law, and since there was no need of danger to life in this, they were forbidden to drive. And at such a time one must not be foolish or cowardly; when there is no aspect of danger to life, one must refuse the order.</p>\n<p>And once a battalion had to carry out an exercise from before dawn until midday. During the exercise there was a long break, but the commander forbade the soldiers to pray Shaḥarit during that break, because he claimed that the brigade commander was due to arrive at any moment for the next stage of the exercise, and as soon as he came they would have to continue the exercise. Since the soldiers knew that the second stage of the exercise would last until midday, and saw that the break had already lasted half an hour, they insisted and prayed Shaḥarit. And they indeed acted according to the law, because there was no necessity at all to continue the exercise immediately and to cancel Shaḥarit on account of this. And if it was possible to wait for the brigade commander for an unlimited time, it was possible to give them time to pray Shaḥarit, for the honour of the brigade commander is not more important than the honour of Heaven.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Gaon Rav Shlomo Goren zt\"l, founder of the military rabbinate and afterwards Chief Rabbi of Israel, served as president of the Har Bracha yeshiva in its first years, and would give a weekly shiur to the young men. Before enlistment in the army he would give the young men preparatory shiurim. In the first shiur he would speak about the value of the mitzva to serve in the army. In the second about keeping Shabbat in the army, and he proved that in time of war the permission is \"until it falls\", which goes beyond danger to life. In the third he would encourage the young men to keep mitzvot in the army with courage, and whenever they encountered an order contradicting the halakha, to refuse, and not to be afraid to pay a personal price for it.<br>And he would tell how he once printed in a booklet of the military rabbinate a halakhic instruction that whenever there is an order to desecrate Shabbat not for the sake of danger to life, one must refuse the order. The Chief of Staff at the time, Ḥayim Laskov, was very angry, and claimed that the army is founded on obedience to orders, and therefore whoever has criticism of any order must first carry it out, and only afterwards, if he wishes, may he submit a complaint against his commander. As punishment for publishing these words, he ordered the publication of the military rabbinate's bulletin to be stopped. Rav Goren did not give in, and complained about the Chief of Staff to Ben-Gurion, who was Prime Minister and Minister of Defence. During the inquiry the Chief of Staff claimed that the whole existence of the army is founded on obedience to orders, and if religious soldiers refuse their commander's order they will harm the foundations of the army's existence. Ben-Gurion turned to Rav Goren with the question: why, then, should the soldier not carry out the order and complain afterwards? Rav Goren answered that desecration of Shabbat is like murder: the forbidden act can no longer be undone afterwards. And just as one who receives an order to kill an innocent person does not kill him and complain about his commander afterwards, so too one who receives an order to desecrate Shabbat does not desecrate it and complain afterwards; rather, he must refuse the order. Rav Goren's words were accepted, and Ben-Gurion rebuked Laskov and even entered a note in his file. And when Rav Goren added that Laskov had cancelled the appearance of the military rabbinate's bulletin, Ben-Gurion ordered that the bulletin continue to be published and even ordered that its number of pages be doubled.<br>Who knows what would have happened had another rabbi been in his place then; perhaps out of great fears he would have claimed that every breach of an order would destroy the army, and therefore the soldiers must always obey every order, even when it plainly contradicts the halakha. We hope that the military rabbinate will continue on the line drawn by its founder, Rav Goren zt\"l.<br>It is told that, with the friendship Rav Goren had with many officers, when matters reached harm to the mitzvot of the Torah, he did not give way. And there were several officers who were dismissed or whose promotion was stopped following harm to the mitzvot of Shabbat and kashrut. His position was that an officer for whom the holiness of the tradition of Israel is not foremost in his concerns is not fit to be a commander in the IDF and to send soldiers to fight for the people of Israel. One battalion commander wanted to test whether his religious soldiers would obey his order, and ordered them to work on the tanks at the time of the final meal before Yom Kippur, and so they entered the fast without having eaten properly. Rav Goren did not agree to any compromise other than the immediate removal of that battalion commander from the army."}]},{"slug":"06-04-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-10/","title_he":"י – חובת המחאה בצבא","title_en":"The Duty of Protest in the Army","body_en":"<p>A soldier whose commanders or comrades have harmed his ability to fulfil one of the mitzvot of the Torah is obligated to complain about it through all the accepted legal means. And this mitzva is more binding than the ordinary mitzva placed upon every one of Israel to rebuke his fellow who sins.</p>\n<p>The mitzva of rebuke must stem from love and responsibility: just as a Jew must take care of his fellow so that he is not hungry, so he must take care that he does not sin. And indeed we find that the mitzva of loving one's fellow and the mitzva of rebuke were said next to each other, as it is said (Vayikra 19:17-18): \"You shall not hate your brother in your heart; you shall surely rebuke your fellow, and not bear sin because of him. You shall not take vengeance, nor bear any grudge against the children of your people, and you shall love your fellow as yourself.\"</p>\n<p>When it is a systemic public sin, the duty of protest increases, for the sin harms the many and the character of society. And so the Sages said (Shabbat 54b): \"Whoever can protest against the members of his household and does not protest is held responsible for the members of his household (punished for the sin of the members of his household); against the people of his city, he is held responsible for the people of his city; against the whole world, he is held responsible for the whole world.\" So a soldier who can protest against a sinful matter and does not protest, even though he himself did not sin, becomes a partner in the sin and is punished (below 7:3, on the severity of the duty of protest and its punishment).</p>\n<p>The duty of rebuke applies even many times, as the Sages said: \"'You shall surely rebuke': even a hundred times\" (Bava Metzia 31a). And this is on condition that there is a chance that the rebuke will be accepted, because the rebuke is directed at a person who accepts the principles on which the protest is based; or when the rebuke is directed at a public body, for when the sum of complaints against it accumulates to a certain amount, it begins to change itself for the better.</p>\n<p>When there is no chance at all that the rebuke will be accepted, it is a mitzva to rebuke once, and beyond that one should not rebuke. As the Sages said (Yevamot 65b): \"Just as it is a mitzva for a person to say something that will be listened to, so it is a mitzva for a person not to say something that will not be listened to\" (Rema OḤ 608:2; MB 9).</p>\n<p>The mitzva of rebuke is binding even when it involves unpleasantness, such as when one must rebuke a person who mistreats his fellow; and even if there is concern that the one receiving the rebuke will punish the rebuker or even want to strike him, one must fulfil the mitzva of rebuke (Rambam, Laws of Character Traits 6:7; below 7:5).</p>\n<p>It follows, then, that the duty of rebuke obliges the soldier even when it involves entering very unpleasant situations with his commanders. The duty exists even when it will most likely not be accepted, for as long as there is a small chance that it will help, there is an obligation to rebuke. And whoever does not rebuke becomes a partner in the sin of the whole chain of command, which harms the Jewish character of the IDF and forces soldiers to act not according to halakha.</p>\n<p>For many religious soldiers it seems that every complaint is considered a challenge to the authority of the commanders, harm to the army and rebellion against the kingdom, as it was in the days of monarchical rule. And even so, the Torah instructed us to protest against the sins of the kingdom. All the more so today, when complaining is considered praiseworthy civic behaviour. For this is how the democratic system involves the whole public in shaping the institutions and improving them.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"<b>How to fulfil the duty of protest:</b> First, one should turn to the direct commander and to the unit's military rabbi. If they solve the problem, good. If the problem is not solved, the protest should be directed to two channels: a) to the office of the commissioner for public inquiries in the Manpower Directorate; parents and family members can also turn there. This office is accustomed to work efficiently and quickly. b) To the open line of the military rabbinate, as the body responsible for matters of Judaism in the IDF.<br>It is advisable to direct the complaint also to the bodies that accompany soldiers in the army, such as the association of hesder yeshivot and the association of higher yeshivot. And likewise to the Soldiers' Complaints Commissioner. This is a body outside the army, whose handling is lengthy and systemic, and which is intended for cases in which the army did not manage to correct itself efficiently. In difficult problems that do not receive a proper response, it is advisable to send a copy to media outlets that wish to help with this.<br>When it is hard for the soldier to fulfil the duty of protest, the parents can replace him and fulfil the duty of protest in his place. The mitzva of rebuke should be fulfilled promptly, because the closer it is to the event, the greater its benefit. But even one who was negligent and did not fulfil it then must fulfil it afterwards, even after a year, because there is still benefit in fulfilling it through all the channels mentioned.<br>Sometimes the soldier who complains may suffer abuse from his commanders and comrades, and then one should consider deferring the complaint until the end of his period of service with them. However, in principle, wherever his complaint is justified, the system should stand by him, and it is worthwhile for him to consult about this with rabbis who know the army.<br>In 2015 the commission received 6,371 complaints, of which 61.06 percent were found justified. Complaints on religious matters were only 0.4 percent of all complaints. One may estimate that if all the religious soldiers complained about religious matters as they complain about other things, a considerable percentage of the complaints in the army would be about religious matters, and presumably the religious state of the IDF would improve beyond recognition. This does not happen because people are negligent in fulfilling the mitzva of rebuke, which is a mitzva from the Torah."}]},{"slug":"06-04-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-11/","title_he":"יא – איסור הקמת יחידות מעורבות ושירות בהן","title_en":"The Prohibition of Establishing Mixed Units and Serving in Them","body_en":"<p>As we have learned (halakha 6), there is a special mitzva to guard modesty in the army; consequently it is clear that it is forbidden to establish in the IDF mixed field units of men and women, and likewise it is forbidden for a soldier to take part in intensive military activity with women.</p>\n<p>And one should not ask: why, then, is a person not forbidden to work in a shop or a bank or any place that men and women enter? For we have learned that in the army one must be more careful in all matters of modesty than in civilian life. In addition, one cannot compare the continuous and close contact in military field units to the contact formed in civilian life. Moreover, the very fact that a person returns every evening to his home, to his family, protects him to a large extent from the evil inclination. Another difference should be noted: the age of military service is younger, and the young men and women are single, and therefore one must increase the fences of modesty more. In addition, in civilian life, every person who feels that his place of work or study may bring him to transgression must leave his place of work or study in order to guard his modesty and holiness. Whereas in the army a soldier has no permission to leave his unit.</p>\n<p>And if they come and claim that the rabbis should educate their students so that they know how to cope with their inclination, we will answer that those who claim this do not understand one of the important foundations of the Torah. The Torah is concerned not only for the righteous but for society as a whole, including those who tend to be easily enticed after their inclination. Therefore fences were set for the mitzvot, and the more central the mitzva, the more numerous the fences around it. For example, the mitzva to believe in God and the prohibition of idolatry are so fundamental that according to the Rambam in the 'Guide for the Perplexed', many of the mitzvot of the Torah serve as a fence against idolatry. And the Sages went in this way: the more important the foundation, the more fences they set around it. The faithfulness and love between spouses too is a sanctified thing, and therefore many fences were set so that a person will keep all his love for his spouse. And even one who is righteous most of his days may one day stumble, and that stumbling may mortally harm his family. And the Sages already said (Ketubot 13b): \"There is no guardian against forbidden relations.\"</p>\n<p>Out of this aim, it is forbidden to agree that only certain units of yeshiva students will be holy, while the rest of the camp will not be holy and unseemly things will be seen in it, because we have responsibility towards every single one of Israel. Therefore we must demand that in all the units of the IDF it will be possible to serve according to halakha. The more soldiers are ready to insist with devotion on their right and duty to serve in every single unit of the IDF according to the rules of halakha, without compromises, the more we will merit that God will walk in the midst of our camp to save us and to give up our enemies before us.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-04-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-12/","title_he":"יב – נשים בצבא","title_en":"Women in the Army","body_en":"<p>Although women too are commanded in saving Israel and in the mitzva of settling the Land, the Torah did not command women to enlist in the army, for it is not their way. As the Sages said, it is not the way of a woman to conquer (Yevamot 65b), and it is not the way of a woman to make war (Kiddushin 2b). Therefore we do not find that women went out to war, neither in the period of the First Temple nor in the period of the Second Temple. And although the Sages said in the Mishna (Sota 44b): \"In wars of mitzva all go out, even a groom from his chamber and a bride from her canopy\", and so ruled the Rambam (Kings 7:4), several commentators explained that since the groom went out of his chamber to war, the bride consequently went out of her canopy and returned to her home. And many explained that since it concerns a war of mitzva, women too are commanded to help the war, but they help in rear roles, supplying water and food (Radbaz, Tiferet Yisrael, Rashash).</p>\n<p>And we have already learned (halakha 6) that in the camp of Israel one must guard oneself from anything immodest, as it is said (Devarim 23:10): \"When you go out as a camp against your enemies, you shall guard yourself from every evil thing.\" And the intention is that one must be careful even about sinful thoughts, as the Sages explained (Avoda Zara 20b): \"You shall guard yourself from every evil thing: that a person should not have thoughts by day and come to impurity at night.\" And so it is implied from the end of the passage that the principal intention is to warn against thoughts of ervah, as it is said (ibid. 15): \"that He see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.\" And from the fact that the Torah used the expression 'ervat <b>davar</b>', they learned that one must be careful about obscene speech and frivolity, for these prohibitions are connected both to speech and to matters of ervah. And from the words of the Sages we learn that crude talk and frivolity may cause the fall of lives of Israel (see Tanḥuma Ki Tetze 3). It follows, then, that the presence of women with men in army conditions is contrary to the guidance of the Torah. And so the Chief Rabbinate of Israel ruled that it is forbidden for girls to enlist in the army.</p>\n<p>But women take part in fulfilling the mitzva by encouraging the men to enlist in the army and by maintaining civilian life in the rear, and in time of need by helping the army in rear roles.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"There is a further prohibition for a woman to wear a man's gear, including weapons. And therefore Ya'el killed Sisera with a tent peg and not with a sword (Nazir 59a). But it seems simple that had she not had a tent peg, she would have had to kill him with a sword, for danger to life overrides this prohibition. And as we have learned, for the sake of saving Israel Ya'el committed a transgression for the sake of Heaven, as explained in Nazir 23b. And likewise in Igrot Moshe OḤ 4:75 he permitted women in time of danger to equip themselves with weapons. And see the words of the Ratziya in 'Le-netivot Yisrael' vol. 1, p. 166 (Beit El edition) in the note, and his position was that women too belong to the mitzva. And see 'Le-or Ha-halakha' by Rav Zevin, pp. 24-27 (Kol Mevaser edition), and in Teḥumin 4 p. 79 an article by Rav Shaviv. And in my humble opinion women are indeed obligated in a war of mitzva to save Israel, as is implied from the plain meaning of the Mishna, and as the words of the Ḥinukh in mitzva 425, concerning the conquest of the Land. But in practice they are exempt for two reasons. a) It is not their way. For their role is to sustain the family, both in raising the coming generations and in the value of life in the present, for if there are no families there is no people and nothing to fight for. In other words, even in time of war the people needs balance, and while the men invest themselves in the war, the women must continue as far as possible to maintain the routine of life in the rear. b) Because the mixing of men and women in the camp harms modesty and holiness, and also harms the concentration required for the war. On the basis of the first foundation it is not fitting that they enlist, and according to the second foundation it is forbidden that they enlist. And these two foundations are hinted at in the words of the Ibn Ezra, Devarim 22:5: \"A man's gear shall not be: this is placed next to going out to war, for the woman was created only to raise seed, and if she goes out with the men she will bring them on the way to licentiousness.\" But since in the foundation of the mitzva women belong like men, the Sages stated it generally in their language: \"In wars of mitzva all go out… and a bride from her canopy.\" And we learn from this that there is a mitzva on them too to help in what is possible within the framework of their feminine role. First, by maintaining the rear and encouraging the men (and perhaps this is why the bride must go out of her canopy, for if she does not go out, the groom too will not go out to war). And when there is a need they will also supply water and food, that is, they will fill rear roles. And if, heaven forbid, a situation arises in which there is a complete necessity for women too to enlist, and without them, heaven forbid, we could not survive, they will be obligated to enlist for war; but it will be necessary to make great efforts to prevent immodest mixing of men and women.<br>In practice, the guidance of the great majority of rabbis and educators, men and women, is that women who keep Torah and mitzvot should not enlist in the army. And although this is not an absolute prohibition like eating meat and milk (our teacher and master the Ratziya too did not agree to say that there is an absolute prohibition in this), since experience shows that enlistment in the army harms the religious level of some of the girls, in practice it leads to many prohibitions; and therefore in terms of its importance, this guidance carries greater weight than that of an ordinary, absolute prohibition. According to the great majority of rabbis, this guidance is general and decisive for all young religious girls, including girls who will clearly not be harmed personally (this guidance was not said to older women in the framework of the standing army). However, there are rabbis, and with them educators, men and women, who, out of close acquaintance with the girls of their circle (a kind of Modern Orthodox), hold that with preparation and accompaniment, in suitable units, there is no concern that they will be harmed. And girls belonging to those communities who want to enlist according to their guidance and after personal counselling, in units where it is possible relatively to keep the rules of modesty, have what to rely on, for Torah guidance depends also on the community and the person. But from the outset it is preferable to conduct oneself according to the great majority of rabbis and not enlist."}]},{"slug":"06-04-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-13/","title_he":"יג – מצווה להציע שלום","title_en":"The Mitzva to Offer Peace","body_en":"<p>When Israel need to open a war against a kingdom or a city, the Torah commanded to call it first to peace, as it is said (Devarim 20:10): \"When you draw near to a city to fight against it, then proclaim peace to it.\" The offer of peace means an offer of surrender, as it is said (ibid. 20:11): \"And it shall be, if it answers you with peace and opens to you, then all the people found in it shall be tributaries to you and shall serve you.\" And the intention is: accepting the rule of Israel, paying taxes and helping with all the needs of Israel. In addition, accepting the universal foundations of faith and morality, which are the seven Noahide mitzvot (Rambam, Kings 6:1). And these are the seven mitzvot: the prohibition of idolatry, the prohibition of forbidden relations, the prohibition of bloodshed, the prohibition of theft and robbery, the prohibition of \"blessing\" the Name (a euphemism, meaning cursing), the prohibition of eating a limb from a living animal, and the obligation to appoint courts that will judge righteous judgement in all the laws between a person and his fellow.</p>\n<p>Even before going out to war to wipe out the seed of Amalek, one must call them to peace, that is, offer them to accept upon themselves the seven Noahide mitzvot, and be subjugated and pay tribute to Israel; and by this they cease to be considered Amalek. If they accepted the terms of peace, one does not fight against them. And if they did not accept, one fights against them until they are destroyed (so it is implied from the Rambam and Kesef Mishneh, Kings 6:1-4; Peninei Halakha Zemanim 14:8).</p>\n<p>And so did Yehoshua. Before he crossed the Jordan, he sent three letters to the Canaanites dwelling in the Land. In the first he sent to them: whoever wants to flee, let him flee. In the second he sent: whoever wants to make peace, let him make peace. And in the third he wrote: whoever wants to make war, we will fight him. And indeed the Girgashites decided to flee, and went to live in Africa, and found a good place there (Yerushalmi Shevi'it 6:1, Devarim Rabba 5:13).</p>\n<p>If so, one would seemingly ask: why did the Giv'onim (Yehoshua 9) need to use cunning and make themselves appear as though they had come from a distant place? According to the Rambam, because they wanted to make a covenant with Israel, whereas the peace Yehoshua offered them was not a covenant, but that they would be tributary and subject to Israel. And since the Giv'onim deceived Israel, they were not obligated to keep the covenant they made with them. But had they broken the oath of the princes, a desecration of the Name would have been caused; therefore they kept the covenant with them. And according to the Ra'avad, the Giv'onim could accept the offer of peace as long as Israel had not crossed the Jordan; but after they crossed the Jordan the war had already begun, and they lost the opportunity to accept the offer of peace (Rambam, Kings 6:5).</p>\n<p>When there is concern that while calling to peace the enemy will attack us and we will suffer losses from it, one should not call it to peace.<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"See 'Ha-tzava Ke-halakha' by Rav Kaufman p. 12, and he wrote in the name of the Netziv that when there is danger, one should not call to peace. We should further note that according to Rashi, following the Sifrei, the mitzva to call to peace applies only in an optional war, whereas according to the Rambam (Kings 6:1) it applies in all kinds of wars."}]},{"slug":"06-04-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-14/","title_he":"יד – מלחמת חורמה","title_en":"A War of Annihilation","body_en":"<p>When Israel's offer of peace is rejected, war is opened. And the war is cruel. As it is said (Devarim 20:12-16): \"And if it will make no peace with you, but makes war against you, then you shall besiege it. And when the Lord your God delivers it into your hand, you shall strike every male in it with the edge of the sword. But the women and the little ones and the cattle and all that is in the city, all its spoil, you shall take as plunder for yourself, and you shall eat the spoil of your enemies which the Lord your God has given you. Thus shall you do to all the cities that are very far from you, which are not of the cities of these nations. But of the cities of these peoples which the Lord your God gives you as an inheritance, you shall save alive nothing that breathes.\"</p>\n<p>One would seemingly ask: why did they need to kill all the males, and of the Canaanites every soul? There are two principal reasons for this. <b>a)</b> In war one must win absolutely, for otherwise another campaign will certainly come that will again endanger our existence, and who knows what its end will be. Not only this, but if the victory is not absolute, we will not deter other enemies from endangering our existence. And everything depends on the custom of the world. In those times this is how victors conducted themselves, and had Israel not conducted themselves as was accepted, all the nations around us would have known that it is worthwhile to fight Israel: if they win, good, and if they lose, it is not so terrible (see Igrot Ha-Re'aya vol. 1, p. 100). <b>b)</b> In terms of justice, the wicked who hate Israel must be punished measure for measure, and what they schemed to do to us must be done to them. And so we did in the days of Haman the wicked.</p>\n<p>And if they ask: if we conduct ourselves so, what will be the difference between Israel and the nations? The difference is twofold: <b>first,</b> we begin with peace; <b>second,</b> even after we win, we strike only as much as is necessary and just, and no more.</p>\n<p>In our day too, we must aspire to absolute victory, until the complete surrender of our enemies, in order to deter and in order to punish. Indeed, thank God, today it is no longer accepted to kill every male, and as a general rule people try not to harm the civilian population, and consequently it is forbidden. But the victory must be strong and painful in a way that will deter our enemies, and it must also contain punishment measure for measure: what they schemed to do to us, we will do to them. But beyond this one should not act.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"Even the civilian population may be harmed in the course of war as is accepted among the nations in times of war, and this is the guidance of the Torah, as it is said (Devarim 20:20): \"And you shall build siegeworks against the city that makes war with you, until it falls.\" And the Sages learned in the Sifrei (there 120, on Devarim 20:12-13): \"'You shall besiege it': even to starve it, even to make it thirst, even to kill it by death from diseases. 'And the Lord your God delivers it into your hand': if you have done all that is said in the matter, in the end the Lord your God will deliver it into your hand.\" Today too this is the custom of the nations, including those considered enlightened. But today it is accepted to limit the harm to the civilian population as much as possible, but without harming the aim of the war, which is: achieving victory, deterrence and just punishment."}]},{"slug":"06-04-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-15/","title_he":"טו – הכרתת רשעים במלחמה","title_en":"Cutting Off the Wicked in War","body_en":"<p>When we go out to war against an enemy who rises against us to destroy us, the aim is not only to save Israel and to deter the haters of Israel, but also to cut off the wicked and wipe them out from the world.</p>\n<p>Indeed, from the outset we wish to do kindness with every person in the world, as Avraham our father did, who welcomed guests. Even worshippers of base idolatry he drew close with love, doing kindness with them through hospitality and drawing them close to faith in God. And the children of Israel are by nature merciful and doers of kindness (Yevamot 79a). But a person has free choice, and when a person has decided to conduct himself with wickedness, to the point of mustering his strength to go out to war against us, to kill among us and to rob from us the holy Land that God gave as an inheritance to our fathers and to us, it is a mitzva to fight him in order to punish him and his supporters.</p>\n<p>As in the words of David our king, who sang (II Shmuel 22:38-43): \"I pursued my enemies and destroyed them, and did not turn back until they were consumed. And I consumed them and crushed them, so that they could not rise; they fell beneath my feet. For You girded me with strength for battle; You subdued under me those who rose against me. And my enemies You made turn their backs to me, those who hate me, and I destroyed them. They looked, but there was none to save; to the Lord, but He did not answer them. Then I beat them as small as the dust of the earth; I crushed them as the mire of the streets, I stamped them down…\" \"The God who executes vengeance for me, and brings down peoples under me\" (ibid. 22:48).</p>\n<p>Indeed, David was not occupied only with defence; he was also occupied with initiated attack on the haters of Israel. It is not fitting to call David's army the \"Israel Defence Forces\", but rather the \"Israel Attack Forces\" or the \"Army of Israel\". And one must know that whoever fights against Israel fights also against the Master of the world, for Israel are His children. Therefore David's wars against the haters of Israel are called \"the wars of the Lord\" (I Shmuel 18:17; 25:28). And this is moral heroism: to wipe out from the world the haters of God and the haters of Israel, and in the merit of this heroism David merited to establish the kingdom of Israel for generations. And so he concluded his song (II Shmuel 22:50-51): \"Therefore I will give thanks to You, O Lord, among the nations, and sing praises to Your name. He is a tower of salvation to His king, and shows kindness to His anointed, to David and to his seed for evermore.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-04-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-16/","title_he":"טז – השאלה על דין אשת יפת תואר","title_en":"The Question of the Law of the Beautiful Captive Woman","body_en":"<p>Seemingly there is a puzzling mitzva in the Torah, according to which soldiers are permitted to take for themselves women from among the daughters of the people fighting against us, as it is said (Devarim 21:10-14): \"When you go out to war against your enemies, and the Lord your God delivers them into your hands, and you take them captive, and you see among the captives a beautiful woman, and you desire her and would take her for yourself as a wife, then you shall bring her into your house, and she shall shave her head and do her nails. And she shall remove the garment of her captivity from upon her, and shall dwell in your house and weep for her father and her mother a full month, and after that you may come to her and be her husband, and she shall be your wife. And it shall be, if you do not desire her, then you shall send her away free, and you shall not sell her at all for money; you shall not treat her as a slave, because you have afflicted her.\"</p>\n<p>To understand the mitzva we must first explain that in ancient days the victors would do to the vanquished whatever they wished. Every kind of abuse was considered legitimate legally and morally. Those who fell captive were considered the property of the victors. Many of them were killed amid public abuse. Therefore King Shaul asked to be killed by his own sword rather than fall captive to the Philistines. Some were slaughtered as sacrifices on the altars of their idols, and some were taken for bloody entertainments and served as gladiators in fights against beasts of prey or among themselves until they were killed.</p>\n<p>The prevailing custom was that a considerable proportion of the men were killed and the rest were sold as slaves. Young and beautiful women had a 'privilege': they were not killed, but at first the soldiers would rape them as they pleased, and when they tired of them they would cast them into a brothel or sell them as maidservants and concubines. This is what everyone used to do, and therefore it was accepted among the nations that when they understood that their defeat was near, the women would adorn themselves so as to find favour in the eyes of the victorious soldiers and thereby save their lives. And a small hope was in the heart: perhaps one of the enemy soldiers would desire her and protect her, because he would want her to serve him as a concubine. And if she merited to bear him a child, presumably he would support her and she would not die of hunger. The parents too helped their daughters adorn themselves, for this was the only chance of saving their daughter, and perhaps in this way their seed would continue to exist in the world. For those who were of no use were generally killed, to reduce the mouths that needed feeding, since many times the wars were over means of livelihood, and therefore one of the aims was to kill the vanquished and inherit their fields.</p>\n<p>In recent centuries, a gradual process of improvement in the legal status of the person began in the developed countries, and together with the abolition of slavery in Europe, the custom of selling the members of the defeated people as slaves and maidservants was abolished. However, until after the Second World War it was still customary to subject prisoners to hard labour for the victorious kingdom or state. Regarding plunder and rape: until the end of the Second World War it was accepted that after the conquest of a city was completed, for three days the law somewhat hid its face, so that the soldiers could plunder and rape women as they pleased, on condition that they did not exaggerate with excessive cruelty. After three days they began to enforce the laws of war against plunder, rape and murder. Only in 1949 was the Fourth Geneva Convention agreed upon, establishing protection for the civilian population in time of war.<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"The First Geneva Convention was established in 1864, in which it was agreed to grant medical treatment to enemy combatants who fell captive. The healthy and recovering soldiers could be put on trial for the very fact of being enemies, and punished with death or hard labour, or as agreed between the two warring sides."}]},{"slug":"06-04-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-17/","title_he":"יז – הלכות אשת יפת תואר","title_en":"The Laws of the Beautiful Captive Woman","body_en":"<p>In light of this we will learn the law of the beautiful captive woman in the Torah: from the outset the soldier must fence himself off from every thought of forbidden relations and think only of saving Israel and of victory in the war (Rambam, Laws of Kings and Their Wars 7:16). And this too is included in the general mitzva to guard the holiness of the camp, as it is said (Devarim 23:10-15): \"When you go out as a camp against your enemies, you shall guard yourself from every evil thing… For the Lord your God walks in the midst of your camp to save you and to give up your enemies before you; therefore your camp shall be holy, that He see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.\"</p>\n<p>So too after the victory, when they would take the women captive according to the accepted custom in ancient days, from the outset the soldier had to fence himself off from every foreign thought. And if despite this he desired one of the captives, the Torah permitted him to come to her once, on condition that he do so out of a commitment to marry her afterwards if she wishes. And some of the Sages hold that he was forbidden to come to her even once, but only to take her with him, and only if she agreed to convert would he be permitted to marry her (the view of R. Yoḥanan and Shmuel), and it is reasonable that this was the guidance from the outset. However, in a situation of after the fact, it was ruled in halakha that the soldier is permitted to come to her once under the stated conditions (Rambam ibid. 8:2, Kesef Mishneh).</p>\n<p>And these are the details of the law: the permission to come to the woman is only in the storm of battle, at the time of taking her from her place into captivity; but once she has already been taken captive, no soldier is permitted to touch any captive woman. And even this permission is on condition that the soldier commits to marry her afterwards, as it is said (Devarim 21:11): \"and you see among the captives a beautiful woman, and you desire her and would take her for yourself as a wife.\" Therefore the soldier is permitted to take only one woman and no more. Likewise he is forbidden to take a woman for his brother or for someone else; only one who desired one particular woman is permitted to take her for himself (Rambam ibid. 8:3). After he has come to her once, he is forbidden to come to her again until the matter of their marriage is settled. That is, if the captive agrees to enter under the wings of the Shekhina, to convert and to marry him, he converts her immediately (but they wait three months until he marries her, so that if she conceives they can know whether she conceived while a gentile or a convert; Rambam ibid. 8:2; 5; 6).</p>\n<p>If she has not yet consented to convert and marry him, he leaves her thirty days to weep and mourn in his house for her father and her mother and for her religion which she must leave, and during that time she shaves her head and grows her nails. And the Rambam added (Guide 3:41) that he even lets her publicly worship the idolatry she was accustomed to worship, without speaking with her about matters of faith during that whole month.</p>\n<p>After that month, if the man no longer wants to marry her, he will require her to accept the seven Noahide mitzvot and release her free, and he is forbidden to subjugate her to himself as a maidservant or to sell her to others, as it is said (Devarim 21:14): \"And it shall be, if you do not desire her, then you shall send her away free, and you shall not sell her at all for money; you shall not treat her as a slave, because you have afflicted her.\" That is, if it became clear that he does not want her, it emerges that by coming to her the first time he afflicted her, and to compensate her he must set her free.</p>\n<p>If after the month he wanted her and she too agreed to convert and marry him, he converts her and marries her. And if she did not want to convert and marry him, he keeps her with him for up to twelve months, for perhaps she will nevertheless consent to him. And if even after twelve months she did not consent, he will require her to accept upon herself the seven Noahide mitzvot and release her free. And she receives the status of a 'ger toshav' (resident alien), who is permitted to live in the Land and with whom it is a mitzva to do kindness, but a man of Israel is forbidden to marry her without a full conversion (Rambam ibid. 8:7).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"06-04-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/06-04-18/","title_he":"יח – ההיתר בטל כיום","title_en":"The Permission Is Void Today","body_en":"<p>From the verses of the Torah we have learned that the law of the beautiful captive woman is a strained permission for a situation of after the fact, that is, for a situation in which, if they do not permit it in this way, people will conduct themselves in a far more severe and harsh way. As the Sages said: \"The Torah spoke only against the evil inclination; better that Israel eat the meat of dying animals that were slaughtered (a doubtful prohibition) and not eat the meat of dying animals that became carcasses (a certain prohibition)\" (Kiddushin 21b). And although the matter was permitted after the fact, the Torah tried to distance us from it as much as possible, and therefore hinted to us in the continuation of the passage that there is great concern that these marriages will not bring blessing, and at times will cause quarrels in the family and disputes over the inheritance, and that the son born of this may be a wayward and rebellious son. As in the words of the Sages, who called this \"a transgression drags another transgression\" (Tanḥuma Ki Tetze 1), and so it is brought in Rashi (Devarim 21:11): \"The Torah spoke only against the evil inclination. For if the Holy One, blessed be He, does not permit her, he will marry her in transgression. But if he married her, in the end he will come to hate her… and in the end he will father from her a wayward and rebellious son; this is why these passages are placed next to each other.\"</p>\n<p>Since this law is for a situation of after the fact, to prevent a more severe situation, today, when it is possible to enforce on the soldiers of Israel the prohibition of harming the occupied population, the permission of the beautiful captive woman is void. And this although in Arab countries and the like it is still accepted that soldiers rape women and murder people, and even in Western armies many soldiers break the law and rape the women of the conquered or ruled people. And so they did in Europe during the First and Second World Wars. In any case, thanks to the beneficial influence of the morality of the Torah, the laws of war among the Western nations have changed for the better, both in terms of the status of the members of the vanquished population, whose lives are not given as property into the hands of the conquerors, and in terms of the ability to enforce military law on the soldiers more effectively thanks to means of communication and transport. Therefore the permission of the beautiful captive woman is void, and the law has returned to its former state: it is forbidden for a man and a woman to have marital relations outside the framework of marriage according to halakha.</p>\n<p>Indeed, we have learned a great foundation from the law of the beautiful captive woman. There is no wisdom in shouting that a certain thing is invalid. One must know how to repair it by planting the foundations of the good within the harsh, cruel and complicated reality, and this is something possible precisely through Israel, as the mitzva of the beautiful captive woman can serve as an example of the desired process of repair.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
